00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
You've learned a lot about Confnet, everything ranging from the architecture of a Confnet,

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.440
to how to use it for image recognition, to object detection, to face recognition,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:17.280
and neural style transfer. And even though most of our discussion has focused on images,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.480
on sort of 2D data, because images are so pervasive, it turns out that many of the

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:31.280
ideas you've learned about also apply not just to 2D images, but also to 1D data as well as to 3D data.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.120
Let's take a look. In the first week of this course, you learn about the 2D convolution,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:44.480
where you might input a 14 by 14 image and convolve that with a 5 by 5 filter. And you

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:55.360
saw how 14 by 14 convolved with 5 by 5, this gives you a 10 by 10 output. And if you had multiple

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:02.960
channels, maybe it was 14 by 14 by 3, then it would be 5 by 5 that matches the same 3,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.400
and then if you have multiple filters, say 16 filters, you end up with 10 by 10 by 16.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:17.680
It turns out that a similar idea can be applied to 1D data as well. For example, on the left is an EKG

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:23.760
signal, also called an electrocardiogram. It's basically if you place an electrode over your

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.760
chest, this measures the little voltages that vary across your chest as your heart beats,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.840
because the little electric waves generated by your heart beating can be measured with a pair

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:43.440
of electrodes. And so this is an EKG of someone's heart beating. So each of these peaks corresponds

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:50.640
to one heart beat. So if you want to use EKG signals to make medical diagnosis, for example,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:58.240
then you would have 1D data, because what EKG data is, is basically a time series showing the

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:05.440
voltage at each instant in time. So rather than a 14 by 14 dimensional input, maybe you just have a

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.600
14 dimensional input. And in that case, you might want to convolve this with a one dimensional

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.360
filter. So rather than the 5 by 5, you just have a five dimensional filter. So with 2D data,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.360
what the convolution allows you to do is to take the same 5 by 5 feature detector and apply it

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.440
at lots of different positions throughout the image. And that's how you wound up with your

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:36.400
10 by 10 output. What a 1D filter allows you to do is take your five dimensional filter,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:43.360
and similarly apply that at lots of different positions throughout this 1D signal. And so if

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:50.560
you apply this convolution, what you find is that a 14 dimensional thing convolved with this five

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.240
dimensional thing, this will give you a 10 dimensional output. And again, if you have

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.400
multiple channels, you might have a 14 by, in this case, EKG is just one channel. If you have a

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:09.760
one lead or one electrode for EKG, so times 5 by 1. And if you have 16 filters, maybe you end up with

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:17.200
10 by 16 over there. And this could be one layer of your continent. And then for the next layer of

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:23.520
your continent, if you input a 10 by 16 dimensional input, and you might convolve that with a

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:30.240
five dimensional filter again, then this needs to have 16 channels. So that has to match. And if you

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:39.280
have 32 filters, then the output of another layer would be 6 by 32 if you have 32 filters.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:45.600
Right. And the analogy to the 2D data is this is similar to how if you have 10 by 10 by 16

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:53.280
data in convolve that would be 5 by 5 by 16. And that has to match that will give you a

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.120
six by six dimensional output. And if you have 32 filters, that's where the 32 comes from.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:05.200
So all of these ideas apply also to 1D data, where you can have the same feature detector,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:11.360
such as this applied to a variety of positions, for example, to detect the different heartbeats

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:18.720
in EKG signal, but to use the same set of features to detect the heartbeats even at different positions

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:25.680
along these time series. And so confidence can be used even on 1D data. For a lot of 1D data

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.040
applications, you actually use a recurrent neural network, which you learn about in the next course,

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:38.000
but some people also can try using confidence in these problems. And in the next course on sequence

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.040
models, which we'll talk about recurrent neural networks and LSTM and other models like that,

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.840
we'll talk about the pros and cons of using 1D confidence versus some of those other models

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:54.960
are explicitly designed for sequence data. So that's the generalization from 2D to 1D.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:01.680
How about 3D data? Well, what is three dimensional data? It is that instead of having a 1D list of

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:10.080
numbers or a 2D matrix of numbers, you now have a 3D block, a three dimensional input volume of numbers.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:17.520
So here's an example of that, which is if you take a CT scan. This is a type of x-ray scan that

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:23.520
gives a three dimensional model of your body. But what a CT scan does is it takes different

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.400
slices through your body. So as you scan through a CT scan, which I'm doing here,

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:34.720
you can look at different slices of the human torso to see how they look. And so this data is

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:41.360
fundamentally three dimensional. And one way to think of this data is if your data now has some

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:49.360
height, some width, and then also some depth where this is where the different slices through

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.840
this volume are the different slices through the torso. So if you want to apply a confident to

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:02.320
detect features in this three dimensional CAT scan or CT scan, then you can generalize the ideas from

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:09.680
the first line to three dimensional convolutions as well. So if you have a 3D volume and for the

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:20.080
sake of simplicity, let's say it's 14 by 14 by 14. And so this is the height, width, and depth of

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:26.880
the input CT scan. And again, just like images don't all have to be square, a 3D volume doesn't

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.840
have to be a perfect cube as well. So the height and width of an image can be different. And in

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.880
the same way, the height and width and the depth of a CT scan can be different. But I'm just using

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:44.000
14 by 14 by 14 here to simplify the discussion. And if you convolve this with a now a five by

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:53.600
five filter, so your filters now are also three dimensional, then this will give you a 10 by 10

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:01.920
by 10 volume. And technically, you could also have by one if this is a number of channels. So this

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.360
is just a 3D volume, but your data can also have different numbers of channels. Then this would be

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:11.440
times one as well because the number of channels here, the number of channels here

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:19.280
has to match. Then if you have 16 filters, the five by five by five by one, then the next

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:26.560
outputs will be 10 by 10 by 10 by 16. And then if so this could be one layer of your

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:32.880
confidence over 3D data. And if the next layer of the confident convolves this again with a

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:39.360
five by five by five by 16 dimensional filter. So this number of channels has to match that as

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:47.040
usual. And if you have 32 filters, then similar to what you saw with confident over images, now you

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:58.400
end up with a six by six by six volume across 32 channels. So 3D data can also be learned on sort

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:05.280
of directly using a three dimensional confidence. And what these filters do is really detect

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:12.400
features across your 3D data. CAT scans, medical scans is one example of 3D volumes.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.960
But another example of data you could treat as a 3D volume would be movie data, where the different

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:25.040
slices could be different slices in time through a movie. And you could use this to detect motion

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:31.920
or people taking actions in movies. So that's it on generalization of confidence from 2D data

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:39.120
to also 1D as well as 3D data. Image data is so pervasive that the vast majority of confidence

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.880
are on 2D data on image data. But I hope that these other models will be helpful to you as well.

